{ Original Scandinavian Defense course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 Qa5 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 c6 6. Bc4 Bf5 7. Bd2 e6 8. O-O Nbd7 { Authored anchor. Black develops the light-squared bishop before closing the diagonal with ...e6. ...c6 gives the queen c7 as a possible retreat and restrains Nb5. } 9. Qe2 Bb4 10. a3 Bxc3 { Plan: Develop the f8 bishop, choose a safe king destination and connect the rooks. Watch for a useful ...c5 or ...e5 break after development. Opponent resource: White has more central space and can play Ne5, Nd5 or d5 when supported. A routine pawn move may lose a crucial queen tempo. } *

[Event "SC-02 White uses Qe2 and long castling"]
[Site "Original study material"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White study side"]
[Black "Black study side"]
[Result "*"]

{ Original Scandinavian Defense course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 Qa5 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 c6 6. Bc4 Bf5 7. Bd2 e6 8. Qe2 Bb4 { Authored anchor. Qe2 prepares long castling and increases central pressure. ...Bb4 develops with a concrete relation to the c3 knight rather than immediately making another pawn move. } 9. a3 Nbd7 10. O-O-O Bxc3 { Plan: Finish development and decide whether the queen should retreat before castling. If kings go to opposite wings, count attack tempi rather than following a fixed pawn storm. Opponent resource: White can play O-O-O and advance the kingside pawns. Opening the center while your own king remains on e8 can be dangerous. } *

[Event "SC-03 White castles short early"]
[Site "Original study material"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
