{ Original Scandinavian Defense course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. c4 Qd8 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 e6 { Authored anchor. The c-pawn move gains a queen tempo but leaves d4 as a possible long-term target. Black retreats safely and develops. } 6. Nc3 c5 7. Be2 cxd4 8. Qxd4 Be7 9. Bf4 O-O 10. Qe3 Ng4 { Plan: Develop the bishops and seek ...c5 or ...Bb4+ when appropriate. Study an isolated d-pawn structure if central exchanges leave White with one. Opponent resource: White has space and easy development. Trying to punish c4 immediately can be worse than accepting a normal position. } *

